    "body": "## Summary\n\nAffects `SMTHHelper.getUnsafeOkHttpClient` in `app/src/main/java/com/zfdang/zsmth_android/newsmth/SMTHHelper.java`.\n\nI found the HTTPS client creating its SSL context with `SSL`, accepting every certificate chain, and bypassing hostname checks. `SSL` is not an allowed protocol value; the allowed values are `TLSV1.2`, `TLSV1.3`, and `TLS`.\n\nI switched the context to `TLS` and replaced the accept-all manager with platform trust plus a bundled ISRG Root X1 fallback. I also removed the custom hostname bypass so OkHttp performs its normal hostname validation. This preserves standard platform trust while supporting the bundled root when needed.\n\nI checked `m.newsmth.net` and `www.newsmth.net`: both chain to ISRG Root X1, which Android trusts only from 7.1.1 (the app supports API 21), so the root is bundled (valid until 2035). `static.newsmth.net` does not resolve from my network, so its chain is unchecked. OkHttp 4.12.0 already negotiates only TLS 1.2 and 1.3 by default, so no protocol filter was added.\n\nI found this while running the app under RVSec, a runtime-verification tool that watches the app's calls to the Java cryptography and TLS APIs and checks each sequence of calls against usage rules written in CrySL. `SSLContextSpec` is the rule this code did not satisfy.\n\n## How was this tested?\n\n- No automated test was added in this change\n- [x] Project compiles successfully (`assembleDebug`)\n\n---\n\n*I'm an undergraduate Computer Engineering student at the University of Brasília (UnB); this contribution is part of a research project on the analysis of Android apps, and I'm happy to adjust it to match the project's conventions.*",
